<TopicPage slug="subjects/library-science/foundational/foundations-of-library-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Library and information science is the discipline of organised access to knowledge. Libraries
    are the visible institutions — public, academic, school, special — but the underlying science
    is information itself: how it is created, described, found, preserved and (crucially) who gets
    to reach it. The ALA's core values — intellectual freedom, privacy, equity of access — are the
    field's constitution.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the mission, the pipeline, the values" widget="equationStep" config={foundationsCfg} />

  <h2>The information life cycle explains the building</h2>
  <p>
    Why does a library have acquisitions, cataloguing, circulation, reference and preservation
    departments? Because information moves through a life cycle, and each stage needs specialists:
    selecting (acquisitions), describing (cataloguing and metadata), lending (circulation),
    answering (reference), and keeping (preservation). The org chart is the pipeline.
  </p>

  <h2>Findability is the core technology</h2>
  <p>
    From Melvil Dewey's 1876 decimal classification to MARC records and modern linked data, the
    field's engineering problem is unchanged: describe things so they can be found again, by people
    who did not create them. Cataloguing rules (AACR2, now RDA), subject headings, classification
    schemes — these are the quiet infrastructure behind every search that works.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the ethics are the hard part</h2>
  <p>
    Libraries hold what people read, and that makes them political: challenges to books,
    surveillance of borrowing records, paywalls versus public access. The profession answers with
    commitments that sometimes cost it — defending offensive books on principle, refusing to hand
    over patron records. Library science teaches that access is a value fought for, not a default.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ALA Core Competences of Librarianship; Rubin &amp; Rubin, <em>Foundations of Library and Information Science</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
